H3={'lifeexp_dyk': "What if where you live decides how long you live? Let's compare life expectancy. Japan: about eighty four years, with almost one hundred thousand people over age one hundred! South Korea: about eighty four, and women there may be the first to average ninety. Spain: about eighty four, and it's predicted to top the world by twenty forty. Switzerland: about eighty four, with some of the longest living men on Earth. Australia: about eighty three, despite the world's deadliest snakes! But here's the twist. America: only about seventy nine, the lowest of all rich countries, even though it spends the most on healthcare, over twelve thousand dollars per person a year! How long do you want to live? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'toilets_dyk': "What if every country had its own toilet rules? Let's take a look. Japan: about eight in ten homes have high tech toilets with a built in bidet, and some even play sounds! Italy: a law from nineteen seventy five says every home needs a bidet. Germany: some older toilets have a little shelf, so you can check before you flush. South Korea: has a toilet theme park, next to a house shaped like a giant toilet! Australia: invented the dual flush toilet in nineteen eighty, saving huge amounts of water. But here's the twist. America: uses about one hundred forty rolls of toilet paper per person a year, among the most on Earth, and public stall doors have big gaps that shock visitors! Bidet or toilet paper? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'tall_dyk': "What if your country decided how tall you grow? Let's compare. The Netherlands: men average about one hundred eighty four centimeters, the tallest on Earth! South Korea: women grew about twenty centimeters in a century, the biggest jump ever recorded. Iran: men grew about sixteen centimeters, the biggest jump for men. Japan: men average about one seventy one, but that's way taller than a hundred years ago. Australia: men average about one seventy nine, taller than most Brits. But here's the twist. America: a century ago, American men were the third tallest in the world, but now they've slipped to around thirty seventh, with an average of about one seventy seven! Are you taller than average? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'tornadoes_dyk': "What if a tornado could hit your town? Let's count tornadoes. The UK: about thirty a year, and for its size it's one of the most tornado packed countries on Earth! Canada: around sixty confirmed a year, the second most in the world. Japan: about twenty five a year, many starting as waterspouts over the sea. Bangladesh: hit by the deadliest tornado ever, in nineteen eighty nine, killing about thirteen hundred people. Australia: dozens a year, plus giant dust devils called willy willies! But here's the twist. America: over twelve hundred tornadoes a year, about three quarters of all the tornadoes reported on Earth, mostly in Tornado Alley! Would you ever chase a tornado? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'starbucks_dyk': "What if there was a Starbucks on every corner? Let's count stores. Italy: Starbucks only arrived in twenty eighteen, because Italians already love their espresso bars. Australia: well under a hundred stores, after closing most of them in two thousand eight! Japan: almost two thousand, including one inside a hundred year old wooden house in Kyoto. South Korea: about two thousand, and Seoul has more Starbucks than New York City. China: over seven thousand, and it keeps growing fast. But here's the twist. America: nearly seventeen thousand stores, about forty percent of all the Starbucks in the world! Coffee shop or homemade coffee? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'weirdlaws_dyk': "What if your country had a law that sounds made up? Let's look. Singapore: selling chewing gum has been banned since nineteen ninety two! Japan: adults aged forty to seventy four get their waist measured at yearly checkups, by law. Denmark: parents must pick a baby name from a government approved list, or ask for special permission. Germany: running out of fuel on the Autobahn can get you fined, because stopping there is banned. Australia: in Queensland, keeping a pet rabbit is illegal, with fines in the tens of thousands! But here's the twist. America: in more than ten states, car dealers aren't allowed to sell cars on Sundays, thanks to old blue laws! Which law would you change? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'coffeeworld_dyk': "What if coffee ran your whole country? Let's compare. Finland: about twelve kilos per person a year, the biggest coffee drinkers on Earth, with coffee breaks written into work contracts! Italy: drinking your espresso standing at the bar is often cheaper than sitting down. Japan: you can buy hot canned coffee from vending machines on almost any street. Brazil: grows about a third of all the world's coffee. Australia: still argues with New Zealand over who invented the flat white! But here's the twist. America: drinks the most coffee in total, about four hundred million cups every single day! How many cups do you drink a day? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'inventions_dyk': "What if you knew who invented the stuff you use every day? Let's look. Australia: helped invent WiFi, Google Maps and the black box flight recorder! Japan: instant noodles, in nineteen fifty eight, and karaoke too. Sweden: the three point seat belt, by Volvo, which gave the design away for free to save lives. Canada: insulin, first used to treat diabetes in nineteen twenty two. The UK: the World Wide Web, by Tim Berners Lee in nineteen eighty nine. But here's the twist. America: the airplane, the first computer network that became the internet, and the smartphone as we know it, all started there! Which invention can't you live without? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'weirdfood_dyk': "What if your dinner was a famous weird food? Let's look. Japan: natto, sticky fermented soybeans with stretchy strings! Sweden: surströmming, fermented herring so smelly that many people open the can outdoors. Iceland: hákarl, shark meat left to ferment and dry for months. South Korea: sannakji, octopus served so fresh it's still wriggling. Australia: witchetty grubs, fat white larvae that taste a bit like nutty egg. But here's the twist. America: at state fairs you can buy deep fried butter on a stick, and even deep fried soda! Which one would you try? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'schools_dyk': "What if your school had a strange rule? Let's look. Japan: students clean their own classrooms and hallways every day, no janitors! Finland: kids start school at seven, and get very little homework. South Korea: many teens study at cram schools so late that Seoul set a ten p m curfew. France: many primary schools have no classes on Wednesday afternoons. Australia: outback kids join the School of the Air, learning from home over radio and now the internet! But here's the twist. America: about half a million yellow school buses, the biggest mass transit fleet in the whole country! What was the weirdest rule at your school? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'music_dyk': "What if your country controlled the music charts? Let's look. Japan: still buys more CDs than almost anywhere, and it's the world's second biggest music market. Sweden: Spotify started in Stockholm, and Swedish producer Max Martin has written over twenty five American number one hits! South Korea: BTS became the first South Korean act to top the Billboard Hot 100. The UK: the Beatles still hold the record for the most Hot 100 number ones, twenty! Australia: AC/DC's Back in Black is one of the best selling albums of all time. But here's the twist. America: the biggest music market on Earth, bringing in roughly a third of all the world's recorded music money! Which artist do you play the most? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'space_dyk': "What if your country sent you to space? Let's count. Russia: Yuri Gagarin became the first human in space in nineteen sixty one, and Valentina Tereshkova the first woman. China: in twenty oh three it became the third country to launch people on its own rockets. Japan: sent the first journalist to space, back in nineteen ninety. Canada: astronaut Chris Hadfield sang a David Bowie song on the space station, and millions watched! Australia: its first astronaut, Andy Thomas, had to become an American citizen to fly with NASA. But here's the twist. America: has sent over three hundred fifty people to space, and it's still the only country that has walked on the Moon, twelve men in all! Would you go to space? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'volcanoes_dyk': "What if a volcano was right next to your house? Let's look. Iceland: about thirty active volcanic systems, on an island of just four hundred thousand people! Italy: Stromboli has been erupting almost nonstop for about two thousand years. Japan: about one hundred eleven active volcanoes, roughly a tenth of the world's total. Indonesia: about one hundred twenty seven active volcanoes, and an eruption in eighteen fifteen caused a year without a summer! Australia: no active volcanoes on the mainland, but Big Ben, on its remote Heard Island, still erupts. But here's the twist. America: about one hundred sixty one potentially active volcanoes, mostly in Alaska, plus the Yellowstone supervolcano! Would you live near a volcano? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'quakes_dyk': "What if the ground shook under your feet every day? Let's look. Japan: gets about a fifth of the world's biggest earthquakes. Chile: had the strongest earthquake ever recorded, magnitude nine point five, in nineteen sixty. New Zealand: records around twenty thousand earthquakes every year. Indonesia: sits on the Ring of Fire, and the twenty oh four quake sent a tsunami across the Indian Ocean. Australia: is moving north about seven centimeters a year, so its maps had to be shifted by almost two meters! But here's the twist. America: Alaska had the second strongest quake ever recorded, and Southern California alone gets about ten thousand earthquakes a year! Have you ever felt an earthquake? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'flags_dyk': "What if your country's flag had a secret story? Let's look. Denmark: has one of the oldest national flags still in use, from the thirteen hundreds. Switzerland: one of only two countries with a square flag, along with Vatican City. Japan: its red sun flag only became the official national flag in nineteen ninety nine! Canada: the maple leaf flag only appeared in nineteen sixty five. Australia: the Aboriginal flag, designed in nineteen seventy one, is also an official flag of the country. But here's the twist. America: today's fifty star flag was designed by a high school student for a class project, and his teacher gave it a B minus! What grade would you give your flag? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'monarchy_dyk': "What if your country still had a king or queen? Let's look. Japan: has the oldest hereditary monarchy in the world, and its emperor has no political power. Denmark: one of the oldest kingdoms in Europe, and its queen gave up the throne in twenty twenty four. Saudi Arabia: one of the last countries where the king holds almost total power. The UK: King Charles is also the king of fourteen other countries! Australia: voted to keep the king as head of state in a nineteen ninety nine referendum. But here's the twist. America: was founded by rejecting a king, and since nineteen fifty one, no president can serve more than two terms! Would you want a king or a president? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'placenames_dyk': "What if your hometown had a name nobody could say? Let's look. New Zealand: a hill with an eighty five letter Māori name, one of the longest place names on Earth! Thailand: the full ceremonial name of Bangkok has over one hundred sixty letters. The UK: a Welsh village with a fifty eight letter name, and its train station sign is a tourist attraction. Germany: a sixty three letter word for a beef labeling law was officially scrapped in twenty thirteen. Australia: has real towns called Nowhere Else and Come By Chance! But here's the twist. America: a lake in Massachusetts has a forty five letter name, and even locals struggle to spell it! Can you say your town's name fast? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'festivals_dyk': "What if your town threw a totally weird festival? Let's look. Spain: La Tomatina, where thousands of people throw over a hundred tons of tomatoes at each other! Thailand: Songkran, a New Year water fight that takes over whole cities. India: Holi, when people cover each other in bright colored powder. The UK: cheese rolling, where people chase a wheel of cheese down a steep hill. Australia: the Melbourne Cup, a horse race so big that Melbourne gets a public holiday! But here's the twist. America: every February, thousands of people wait for a groundhog named Phil to predict the weather, a tradition since eighteen eighty seven! Which festival would you join? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'roads_dyk': "What if your road trip broke a world record? Let's look. China: has the world's biggest expressway network, about one hundred eighty thousand kilometers. Norway: the Lærdal Tunnel runs over twenty four kilometers through a mountain, the longest road tunnel on Earth. Turkey: has the world's longest suspension bridge span, over two kilometers. Japan: some roads play music when you drive over them at the right speed! Australia: the Eyre Highway has a dead straight stretch of about one hundred forty six kilometers, with no turns at all! But here's the twist. America: has the largest road network on Earth, over six and a half million kilometers, enough to reach the Moon and back about eight times! Road trip or plane? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'themeparks_dyk': "What if you lived next to the world's biggest theme park? Let's look. Denmark: Bakken, from fifteen eighty three, is the oldest amusement park still open! Japan: Tokyo Disneyland, opened in nineteen eighty three, was the first Disney park outside America. France: Disneyland Paris is the most visited tourist attraction in Europe. The UAE: Formula Rossa at Ferrari World hits two hundred forty kilometers an hour, one of the fastest roller coasters ever. Australia: the Gold Coast has five big theme parks close together! But here's the twist. America: Disney's Magic Kingdom in Florida is the most visited theme park on Earth, with about seventeen million guests a year! Roller coaster or water slide? Comment below, and subscribe for more!"}
